Basic debt calculations
The equations governing loan-related calculations are as follows. The annual repayment X, on a loan of principal amount P, term T years, at interest rate r (expressed as a fraction), is given by

X = Pr\frac{R^T}{R^T-1}
\tag{1}
where R=1+r.
The outstanding debt D, on a loan of principal amount P, term T, at interest rate r, after N years is given by

D = P\left[R^N-\frac{R^T(R^N-1)}{R^T-1}\right]
\tag{2}
All amounts determined in the model are annual amounts, so, for example current-income is the income in the current year, not aggregate income over the five-year period which the current model tick represents.
This means we can calculate debt repayments as an annual amount (based on eqn.1 above) and simply deduct this from the profit determined as described in the income and costs page. Outstanding debt to pay is always determined from eqn.2 not by accumulating repayments over time, thus avoiding issues that might arise due to floating point calculations leaving small amounts owing or overpaid. Instead a farmer’s debt-maturity parameter is incremented by 5 years each model tick, and eqn.2 is used to determine the current amount still owing. When debt-maturity exceeds the mortgage-term it is assumed that all debt has been paid and the debt-free? flag is set true.
